right now all eyes are on Wisconsin  where the voters are determining whether  to key incumbent Governor Scott Walker  or replace him with Milwaukee Mayor Tom  Barrett many see this election as a  referendum not just for you know  governor Scott Walker but also the  policies that he tried to implement to  curb public employee unions negotiating  power over their contracts because of  the national ramifications of what's  going on Wisconsin the reason root poll  actually went into Wisconsin and survey  about 700 Wisconsin residents to find  out what they think about the  controversial law and also how they plan  to vote on june fifth  part of Wisconsin act 10 was to ask  public employee unions to increase what  they contribute to their own health care  from six percent to twelve percent to  the cost of their health care  overwhelmingly Wisconsin voters approve  of this provision  a lot of polls in 2011 came out asking  people if they favor or opposed reducing  collective bargaining rights of public  employee unions and overwhelmingly  people said no we asked the same  question but didn't use the word rights  all we said was would you favor or  oppose limiting collective bargaining  the results flip and then a majority  favored limiting collective bargaining  interestingly Governor Walker exempted  public safety unions those you know  firefighters and police officers from  some of the most controversial aspects  of the law so we asked wisconsin'  residents what they thought and a  majority opposed exempting them from the  law  proponents of the law say that public  employee unions have too much political  power and thus to reform public employee  unions that power needs to be limited so  we asked Wisconsin residents if public  employee unions when negotiating their  contracts have too much power not enough  power or about the right amount and we  found a plurality said that public  employee unions had too much power and  negotiating their contracts however a  plurality is not a majority and in part  this is indicative of many people not  knowing what collective bargaining  actually is and if you don't know what  it is it's hard to determine if someone  has too much power these results tell us  that Wisconsin voters aren't quite ready  to oust Governor Scott Walker from  office in fact fifty percent plan to  vote for him on june fifth this is  especially interesting given that  President Barack Obama leads Mitt Romney  in the state by 10 percentage points in  the presidential election in fact  quarter of those who plan to vote for  scott walker have a favorable opinion of  president obama the fact that many  Walker supporters also support President  Barack Obama demonstrates that the  desire to reform public employee unions  extends beyond partisan lines
 

